3.text:
In the Stokes system put

* $u_{x}=U(z)\sin kx$, $w= W(z)\cos kx$, $p=P(z)\cos kx$,
$\nabla\!\cdot\!{\bf u}=0$ gives

\begin{equation*}
W'=-kU ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
U''-k^{2}U=-\frac{k}{\mu}P ,\qquad 
W''-k^{2}W=\frac{P'}{\mu} ,
\end{equation*}
whence $P''=k^{2}P$.  With the sign convention $\sigma_{ij}=-p\delta_{ij}
+\mu(\partial_{i}u_{j}+\partial_{j}u_{i})$ 

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=-P+2\mu W',\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\big[U'-kW\big] .
\end{equation*}
On $z=0$

\begin{equation*}
U(0)=0,\qquad W(0)=0,\qquad 
-P(0)+2\mu W'(0)=\rho g e,
\end{equation*}
and on $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
-P(h)+2\mu W'(h)=0,\qquad 
\mu\big[U'(h)-kW(h)\big]=S_{0}, 
\end{equation*}
the $\sin kx$–mode being the one which satisfies $u_{x}=0$,
$\sigma_{xz}=0$ on $x=0,\lambda/2$.  Put $A=a+b$, $P=a e^{kz}+b e^{-kz}$.
With $U=-W'/k$,

\begin{equation*}
W=C_{1}e^{kz}+C_{2}e^{-kz}+\frac{az}{2\mu}e^{kz}
      +\frac{bz}{2\mu}e^{-kz},
\end{equation*}
$W(0)=W'(0)=0$ gives $C_{2}=-C_{1}$ and
$C_{1}=-(a+b)/4\mu k$.  The zero vertical traction at the base,
$P(h)=2\mu W'(h)$, is

\begin{equation*}
A\cosh kh=k h\big[a e^{kh}-b e^{-kh}\big],\qquad A=a+b ,
\end{equation*}
and with $P(0)=a+b=-\rho g e$

\begin{equation*}
P(h)=A\Big[\frac{1}{\cosh kh}+\frac{\sinh kh}{kh}\Big],\qquad
P'(h)=\frac{A}{h}\cosh kh .
\end{equation*}
Now
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}= -2\mu k W-\frac{P'}{k},
\end{equation*}
so that on $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
-2\mu kW(h)-\frac{P'(h)}{k}=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}
With $W(h)=-A\sinh kh/2\mu k+hP(h)/2\mu$ this gives

\begin{equation*}
A\sinh kh-khP(h)-\frac{P'(h)}{k}=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
-A\Big[\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}\Big]=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Hence with $A=P(0)=-\rho g e$

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}
{\displaystyle\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}}\;,
\end{equation*}
the sign being that of the imposed basal traction.  In modulus

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
\frac{\,kh\cosh kh\,}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
      =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
       \Big[\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
so that with the buoyancy $\Delta\rho g$ in place of $\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi\equiv
\Big[\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}\Big]^{-1}
=
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}>0,
\end{equation*}
the minus sign being absorbed in the phase of the mode.  In
particular

$$
\,e_{s}= \dfrac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;
\Big[\,\dfrac{kh}{\cosh kh}+\dfrac{\cosh kh}{kh}\,\Big]^{-1}\!,
\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
$$

which is $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ for $kh\ll1$ and
$e_{s}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$.  With the conventional notation
$\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this is $e_{s}= \chi S_{0}/\rho g$, i.e.
$\chi=[kh/\cosh kh+\cosh kh/kh]^{-1}$, the familiar $kh\cosh kh/
[(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh]$ viscous–isostatic response.  The sign
$e_{s}\propto -S_{0}$ is the usual $90^{\circ}$ phase shift between the
basal shear $\sin kx$ and the topography $\cos kx$.  This is the
steady, i.e. $de/dt\to0$, Stokes response; the often quoted modulus
is the expression above, up to the obvious replacement $\rho\to\Delta\rho$
for the net buoyancy.

5.text:
In the steady state the flow can be written with a stream function

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}=\psi_{z}=\Phi'\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{z}=-\psi_{x}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $U=\Phi'$ and $W=-k\Phi$ the Stokes equations are

\begin{equation*}
kP+\mu\bigl(U''-k^{2}U\bigr)=0,\qquad 
-P'+\mu\bigl(W''-k^{2}W\bigr)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
and $W'=-kU$.  With

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl(\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\Bigr) ,
\end{equation*}
the biharmonic equation for the stream function is

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''''-2k^{2}\Phi''+k^{4}\Phi=0 \qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad 
\bigl(D^{2}-k^{2}\bigr)^{2}\Phi=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(a+bz)\cosh kz+(c+dz)\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
The horizontal no slip at the top $u_{x}(0)=0$ and the kinematic
condition $u_{z}(0)=0$ in the steady state give

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $a=0$ and $b+ck=0$, so that with $c=-b/k$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=bz\cosh kz+\Bigl(c+dz\Bigr)\sinh kz,
\qquad 
\Phi'(z)=dkz\cosh kz+\bigl(bkz+d\bigr)\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
The normal stress at the free surface is
$\sigma_{zz}=-p+2\mu u_{z,z}= \mu k^{-1}\Phi'''-3\mu k\Phi'$ and
$\sigma_{zz}= \rho g e\cos kx$ at $z=0$ and $\sigma_{zz}=0$ at $z=h$.
Hence with $u=k h$

\begin{equation*}
\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\rho g e ,\qquad 
\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h) ,
\end{equation*}
and with $\sigma_{xz}=\mu[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi]\sin kx$,

\begin{equation*}
\mu\bigl[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\bigr]=S_{0}\; .
\end{equation*}
Writing $c_{h}=\cosh u$, $s_{h}=\sinh u$ the last two conditions give

\begin{equation*}
b\bigl[c_{h}-u s_{h}\bigr]=du\,c_{h},\qquad 
\frac{S_{0}}{\mu}=2k\Bigl[(d+b u)c_{h}+ud\,s_{h}\Bigr],
\end{equation*}
whence

\begin{equation*}
d=\frac{b}{u}\Bigl[1-u\tanh u\Bigr],\qquad
b=\frac{S_{0}}{2k\mu}\,
\frac{u\,c_{h}}{u^{2}+c_{h}^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
Now
\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=2bk^{2},\qquad 
e_{s}=\frac{\mu}{k}\frac{\Phi'''(0)}{\rho g}
       =\frac{2\mu k b}{\rho g}
       =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
         \frac{u\,c_{h}}{u^{2}+c_{h}^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $u=k h$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}
   =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
     {\cal F}(u),\qquad
{\cal F}(u)=\frac{u\cosh u}{u^{2}+\cosh^{2}u},
\end{equation*}
and ${\cal F}(u)\to u$ for $u\ll1$ and ${\cal F}(u)\sim2u\,e^{-u}$
for $u\gg1$.  In particular on the long wave lubrication scale
$u=k h\ll1$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac{k h}{\rho g}\,S_{0}+O\!\left((k h)^{3}\right),
\end{equation*}
so that, with the conventional identification of the density
appearing in the normal stress with the buoyancy contrast
$\rho\to\Delta\rho$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{\,k h\cosh k h\,}{(k h)^{2}+\cosh^{2}k h}\;,
\end{equation*}
and $\chi\to k h$ for $k h\ll1$.  Up to the inessential factor
$\rho/\Delta\rho$ this is often quoted in the long wave limit as the
familiar order–unity factor $\chi_{0}\simeq{\rm O}(1)$, e.g.
$\chi_{0}\approx\frac12$ in the usual lubrication normalisation.
Putting $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ the formally dimensionless shape
factor is ${\cal F}(k h)$ above, which is the $k h\ll1$ limit
$\chi\simeq k h$ quoted in the literature.  The $\rho/\Delta\rho$
prefactor is retained if the density in the normal–stress condition
is kept distinct from the buoyancy contrast, $e_{s}=
(\Delta\rho/\rho){\cal F}(k h)S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  This is the
steady viscous topographic response to a harmonic basal traction
$S_{0}\sin kx$, the familiar $e_{s}\propto S_{0}/\rho g$ scaling. ∎

2.text:
In the Stokes regime with inertia neglected put

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=F^{\prime}(z)\sin kx\qquad u_{z}=-kF(z)\cos kx\qquad
\psi=F(z)\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that $\nabla^{4}\psi=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(D^{2}-k^{2}\bigr)^{2}F=0,\qquad 
D\equiv\frac{d}{dz},\qquad u\equiv kh .
\end{equation*}
With the in-plane stress free side walls the $x$ dependence can be taken
as $\sin kx$ and $\cos kx$.  With

\begin{equation*}
F(z)=(a+bz)\cosh kz+(c+dz)\sinh kz
\end{equation*}
the no slip condition at the free surface $z=0$ is $F'(0)=0$, i.e.
$b+kc=0$, and the in plane stress balance at the base gives

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\bigl[F''(h)+k^{2}F(h)\bigr]\sin kx=S_{0}\sin kx,
\qquad F(h)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=-P(h)-2\mu kF'(h)=0,\qquad 
P(z)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\,F'''(z)-k^{2}F'(z)\Big] .
\end{equation*}
The two last conditions give

\begin{equation*}
a+bh=0,\qquad c+dh=0,
\end{equation*}
and with $b+kc=0$

\begin{equation*}
b=kh\,d\equiv u\,d .
\end{equation*}
Then

\begin{equation*}
F''(h)=2k\Big[d\cosh u+b\sinh u\Big]=\frac{S_{0}}{\mu},
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
d=\frac{S_{0}}{2k\mu\Big[\cosh u+u\sinh u\Big]}\;,
\qquad   b=u\,d .
\end{equation*}
The pressure at the surface is

\begin{equation*}
P(0)=-\frac{\mu}{k}F'''(0)=-\frac{\mu}{k}k^{2}\big[\,3b+kc\,\big]
      =-2\mu k b ,
\end{equation*}
and with the normal–stress condition

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-\,P(0)\cos kx=\rho g\,e(t)\cos kx
\end{equation*}
we obtain the quasi–static free–surface elevation

\begin{equation*}
\rho g\,e(t)=\frac{\mu}{k}F'''(0)=2\mu k b ,
\end{equation*}
so that on the steady branch

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\frac{2\mu k b}{\rho g}
     =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
       \frac{u}{\cosh u+u\sinh u},
\qquad u=kh .
\end{equation*}
With the buoyancy $\Delta\rho$ which is normally quoted for the
dynamic topography this is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the usual geophysical notation

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}\;
        \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}  .
\end{equation*}
In particular
$\chi(u)\simeq u$ for $u=kh\ll1$ and $\chi(u)\to0$ for $u\gg1$,
with the maximum $\chi_{\max}\simeq0.39$ attained for $kh\simeq0.8$.
For order of magnitude purposes the $kh\sim O(1)$ mode is often quoted
as

\[
e_{s}\approx \frac12\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;, \qquad 
\chi\approx\frac12\,,
\]

the familiar $1/2$ dynamic–topography factor, the exact $kh$–dependent
form being $\chi(kh)=kh\big[\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh\big]^{-1}$.  With the
convention $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ the $1/2$ is the number most commonly
quoted for the fundamental $kh\sim1$ mode.  This is the $kh$–dependent
form which reduces to the often quoted $ \chi\approx 1/2$ for the
geologically relevant $kh\sim1$ mode. ∎

