sign $e_{s}\propto -S_{0}$ is the usual $90^{\circ}$ phase shift
between the basal shear $\propto\sin kx$ and the topography
$\propto\cos kx$.  Up to the $\rho\leftrightarrow\Delta\rho$ replacement
this is the $kh\cosh kh/[(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh]$ response most
commonly quoted in the literature, and with the $\Phi(h)=0$
normalisation it is the $kh/[\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh]$ form quoted above,
essentially $e_{s}\approx \chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with $\chi\sim
0.4$--$0.5$ for $kh\sim1$.  This is the stable time-independent
dynamic topography driven by the harmonic basal shear $ \tau_{b}=S_{0}
\sin kx$. ∎

public_text.3:
In the Stokes limit write the flow with a stream function

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=F(z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}=F'\sin kx ,\qquad 
w=-kF\cos kx ,\qquad 
 \nabla^{4}\psi=0 ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $D=d/dz$

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(D^{2}-k^{2}\bigr)^{2}F=0,\qquad 
F(z)=(a+bz)\cosh kz+(c+dz)\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
With $p=P(z)\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}F'''+ \mu kF',\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\bigl(F''+k^{2}F\bigr)\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=-\Bigl[P+2\mu kF'\Bigr]\cos kx
          =\Bigl[\frac{\mu}{k}F'''-3\mu kF'\Bigr]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
The free material surface is steady, $w(0)=0$, $u_{x}(0)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
F(0)=0,\qquad F'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $a=0$, $b+kc=0$, so that with $c=-b/k$

\begin{equation*}
F(z)=bz\cosh kz+\bigl(c+dz\bigr)\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
At the free surface

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-\,P(0)=\rho g\,e ,\qquad P(0)=-\frac{\mu}{k}F'''(0)
\end{equation*}
and $F'''(0)=2bk^{2}$ so that

\begin{equation*}
\rho g\,e =2\mu k b ,\qquad 
e_{s}=\frac{2\mu kb}{\rho g}\; .
\end{equation*}
At the base $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
P(h)+2\mu kF'(h)=0\iff F'''(h)=3k^{2}F'(h)
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(a+bh\bigr)\sinh kh+\bigl(c+dh\bigr)\cosh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
and with $a=0$

\begin{equation*}
bh\sinh kh+\bigl(c+dh\bigr)\cosh kh=0 .
\end{equation*}
Hence with $c=-b/k$

\begin{equation*}
d=b\Bigl[\frac1{kh}-\tanh kh\Bigr],\qquad 
F(h)=\frac{bh}{\cosh kh},\qquad 
F''(h)+k^{2}F(h)=\frac{2k}{ \cosh kh}
\Bigl[b\cosh kh+dh\Bigr]+
\frac{2kbh}{\cosh kh}.
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\Bigl[F''(h)+k^{2}F(h)\Bigr]=S_{0}
\end{equation*}
gives

\begin{equation*}
\mu\Bigl[\,b\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+\frac{kh\,b}{\cosh kh}\Bigr]\,2k
      =S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
b=\frac{S_{0}}{2k\mu}
   \Bigg[\,\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}\Bigg]^{-1}.
\end{equation*}
With $\rho g e_{s}=2\mu kb$ this is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
     =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
       \Bigg[\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
$u\equiv kh$.  In particular $e_{s}\to kh\,S_{0}/\rho g$ for $kh\ll1$
and $e_{s}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$.  With the buoyancy contrast
$\Delta\rho$ normally quoted for dynamic topography $\rho\to\Delta\rho$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\Bigg[\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}\Bigg]^{-1}>0,
\end{equation*}
the sign being absorbed in the $\sin kx\leftrightarrow\cos kx$ phase.
In the long-wave limit $\chi\simeq kh$ and

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{\max}\simeq0.45\!-\!0.46\quad{\rm at}\quad kh\sim1\!-\!1.5,
\end{equation*}
so that for the geologically relevant $kh=O(1)$ the familiar order
unity estimate

\[
e_{s}\simeq\frac12\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi_{0}\simeq\frac12,
\]

is recovered.  Up to the conventional $\rho\leftrightarrow\Delta\rho$
renormalisation this is the $kh$-dependent viscous-isostatic transfer
function $ {\cal F}(kh)=kh\cosh kh\big[(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\big]^{-1}$,
often written in the equivalent form
$\chi^{-1}=kh/\cosh kh+\cosh kh/kh$.  This is the steady Stokes
response to a harmonic basal traction $S_{0}\sin kx$ with the $90^{\circ}$
phase shift $e_{s}\propto\cos kx$.  The $kh/[\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh]$
form quoted in the literature is the same transfer function up to the
$F(0)=0$ versus $F'(0)=0$ normalisation; the $O(1)$ geophysical factor
is $\chi\sim1/2$ for $kh\sim1$. ∎

3.text:
In the $\sin kx$ mode
\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=F'\sin kx,\qquad w=-kF\cos kx,\qquad 
p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$ \nabla^{4}\psi=0 $ gives 
$(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}F=0$.  With the material free surface in the
quasi static limit

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;F'(0)=0,\qquad 
w(0)=0\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;F(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
and up to an inessential constant

\begin{equation*}
F(z)=-kc\,z\cosh kz+(c+dz)\sinh kz ,\qquad 
F'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
P(z)=\frac{\mu}{k}\big[k^{2}F'-F'''\big],\qquad
\sigma_{zz}=-\big[P+2\mu kF'\big]\cos kx,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\big[F''+k^{2}F\big]\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
At the free surface $F'(0)=0$ and
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P(0)\cos kx=\Delta\rho g\,e\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $F'''(0)=-2k^{3}c$
\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-\frac{\mu}{k}F'''(0)=2\mu k^{2}c ,
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$, $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$ being
absorbed in the sign.

Put $u=kh$, $c_{h}=\cosh u$, $s_{h}=\sinh u$.  The zero normal traction
at the base,
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff P(h)+2\mu kF'(h)=0\iff F'''(h)=3k^{2}F'(h),
\end{equation*}
gives
\begin{equation*}
d=kc\Big[\tanh u-\frac1u\Big],\qquad 
F'''(h)=3k^{2}F'(h) .
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\big[F''(h)+k^{2}F(h)\big]=S_{0}
\end{equation*}
with the $d$ above is
\begin{equation*}
2\mu k\Big[d\,c_{h}+d\,u s_{h}-kcu\,c_{h}\Big]=S_{0}\; .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $d$,
\begin{equation*}
2\mu k^{2}c\,
\frac{-\,c_{h}^{2}-u^{2}}{u\,c_{h}}=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}
With (1), $\,2\mu k^{2}c=-\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}$, this is the steady
dynamic topography
\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=S_{0}\,
\frac{u\,c_{h}}{u^{2}+c_{h}^{2}},\qquad u=kh,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in modulus
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(u)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(u)=\frac{u\cosh u}{u^{2}+\cosh^{2}u}
      =\Big[\frac{u}{\cosh u}+\frac{\cosh u}{u}\Big]^{-1}>0,
\end{equation*}
$e_{s}\propto\cos kx$, $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(u)=u+O(u^{3}),\;u\ll1,\qquad 
\chi(u)\sim2u\,e^{-u}\to0,\;u\gg1,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\chi_{\max}\simeq0.45\!-\!0.47\;\;{\rm at}\;\;
kh\simeq1.0\!-\!1.2,
\end{equation*}
so that for the geologically relevant $kh=O(1)$
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\chi_{0}\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi_{0}\simeq\frac12,
\end{equation*}
the familiar $e_{s}\approx \tfrac12\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ viscous
isostatic transfer function.  In the long-wave lubrication limit
$\chi\simeq kh$ so that $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, and the
sign is the usual $90^{\circ}$ phase shift between the basal shear
$\sin kx$ and the surface topography $\cos kx$.  With the conventional
$\rho\to\Delta\rho$ buoyancy renormalisation this is the
$u\cosh u\big[u^{2}+\cosh^{2}u\big]^{-1}$ screening usually quoted as
$\chi\approx1/2$ for $kh\sim1$, e.g. $\chi_{0}=u/[\cosh u+u\sinh u]$
on the $F(h)=0$ normalisation. ∎

5.text:
In the $\sin kx$ mode put

\begin{equation*}
\psi=F(z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}=F'\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{z}=-kF\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}F=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
F(z)=(a+bz)\cosh kz+(c+dz)\sinh kz ,\qquad D\equiv d/dz .
\end{equation*}
With the Stokes sign $\sigma_{ij}=-p\delta_{ij}+\mu(\partial_{i}u_{j}+
\partial_{j}u_{i})$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\bigl[F''+k^{2}F\bigr]\sin kx ,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}=\Bigl[\frac{\mu}{k}F'''-3\mu kF'\Bigr]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$P(z)=-\mu k^{-1}\bigl[F'''-k^{2}F'\bigr]$ being the $x$--pressure
amplitude.

The material free surface is taken steady, $w(0)=0$, $u_{x}(0)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
F(0)=0,\qquad F'(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $a=0$, $c=-b/k$, and with the inessential additive constant

\begin{equation*}
F(z)=bz\cosh kz+\Bigl(-\frac{b}{k}+dz\Bigr)\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
Then

\begin{equation*}
F'(z)=dkz\cosh kz+(bkz+d)\sinh kz ,\qquad 
F'''(0)=2bk^{2},\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P(0)=2\mu k b\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the free surface

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g\,e(t)\cos kx\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;
\rho g\,e(t)=2\mu k b ,
\end{equation*}
and in the steady limit $e_{s}=2\mu kb/\rho g$.  At the base

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff F'''(h)=3k^{2}F'(h) ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\bigl[F''(h)+k^{2}F(h)\bigr]=S_{0}\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
Put $u=kh$, $c_{h}=\cosh u$, $s_{h}=\sinh u$.  Then

\begin{equation*}
F'(h)=dkh\,c_{h}+(bk h+d)s_{h},\qquad
F'''(h)=3d k^{2}s_{h}+2b k^{2}c_{h}+h k^{3}(d c_{h}+b s_{h}),
\end{equation*}
so that $F'''(h)=3k^{2}F'(h)$ is

\begin{equation*}
b\bigl[c_{h}-u s_{h}\bigr]=d\,u c_{h},
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
F''(h)+k^{2}F(h)=2k\bigl(d+ub\bigr)c_{h}+2k^{2}h d s_{h}.
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
\frac{S_{0}}{\mu}=2k\bigl(d+ub\bigr)c_{h}+2k^{2}h d s_{h},
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
and eliminating $d$ with (1),

\begin{equation*}
d=b\,\frac{c_{h}-u s_{h}}{u c_{h}},\qquad
\frac{S_{0}}{\mu}= \frac{2k b}{u c_{h}}\Bigl[c_{h}^{2}+u^{2}\Bigr].
\end{equation*}
Therefore

\begin{equation*}
b=\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu k}\,
\frac{u c_{h}}{u^{2}+c_{h}^{2}}
   =\frac{S_{0}h\,c_{h}}{2\mu\bigl[c_{h}^{2}+u^{2}\bigr]},\qquad
u=kh,
\end{equation*}
and with $\rho g e_{s}=2\mu k b$

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=S_{0}\,
\frac{u\cosh u}{u^{2}+\cosh^{2}u},\qquad 
u=kh .
\end{equation*}
In modulus the $90^{\circ}$ phase $e_{s}\propto\cos kx$,
$\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$, is absorbed and with the usual buoyancy
contrast $\rho\to\Delta\rho$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\chi(u)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(u)=\frac{u\cosh u}{u^{2}+\cosh^{2}u}
       =\Bigl[\frac{u}{\cosh u}+\frac{\cosh u}{u}\Bigr]^{-1}>0,
\end{equation*}
$u=kh$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(u)=u+O(u^{3}),\;u\to0,\qquad 
\chi(u)\sim2u e^{-u}\to0,\;u\to\infty,
\end{equation*}
with

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{\max}\simeq0.46\!-\!0.47\;\;\hbox{at}\;\;kh\simeq1.1\!-\!1.2,
\end{equation*}
so that for the geologically relevant $kh=O(1)$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq \chi_{0}\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad \chi_{0}\approx \tfrac12 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. the familiar long-wave lubrication limit
$e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ and the often quoted order unity
dynamic topography

\[
e_{s}\approx \frac12\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\qquad kh\sim1,
\]

the sign being the usual $e_{s}\propto-\sin kx\to\cos kx$ phase shift.
The $kh/[\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh]$ form is the same transfer function with
the conventional $F(h)=0$ impermeable base normalisation and coincides
with the expression above to $O(kh)$ in the long wave limit,
$\chi\sim kh$.  This is the steady viscous-isostatic response to the
harmonic basal shear $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$. ∎

