$w=-kF\cos kx$,

$(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}F=0$ and with the Stokes sign
$\sigma_{xz}= \mu\,[F''+k^{2}F]\sin kx$,
$\sigma_{zz}= \mu k^{-1}[F'''-3k^{2}F']\cos kx$, $P=-\mu k^{-1}
[F'''-k^{2}F']$, up to an additive constant

\begin{equation*}
F(z)=(a+bz)\cosh kz+(c+dz)\sinh kz\,,
\end{equation*}
and on the material free surface in the quasi static limit
$F(0)=0$, $F'(0)=0$,
$a=0$, $b+kc=0$.  With $b=-kc$,

\begin{equation*}
F(z)=-kc\,z\cosh kz+(c+dz)\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
$F'(0)=0$, $w(0)=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
F'''(0)=-2k^{3}c ,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}F'''(0)\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the steady branch with the buoyancy contrast
$\Delta\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}= -\frac{\mu}{k}F'''(0)=2\mu k^{2}c ,
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ phase $e_{s}\propto\cos kx$, $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$,
being absorbed in the sign.

Put $u=kh$, $c_{h}=\cosh u$, $s_{h}=\sinh u$.  The zero normal traction
at the base

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff F'''(h)=3k^{2}F'(h)
\end{equation*}
gives with $b=-kc$

\begin{equation*}
 d\,hC_{h}=c\big[\,U S_{h}-C_{h}\,\big],\qquad
d=kc\,\frac{U S_{h}-C_{h}}{UC_{h}},
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\big[F''(h)+k^{2}F(h)\big]=S_{0}
\end{equation*}
is

\begin{equation*}
 S_{0}=2\mu k^{2}c\,\frac{-\,U^{2}-C_{h}^{2}}{UC_{h}}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
c=-\frac{S_{0}\,U C_{h}}{2\mu k^{2}\big[U^{2}+C_{h}^{2}\big]}\;.
\end{equation*}
With (1)

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=S_{0}\,
\frac{U\,C_{h}}{U^{2}+C_{h}^{2}},\qquad U=kh,
\end{equation*}
so that in modulus, the $\sin kx\leftrightarrow\cos kx$ phase being
absorbed,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}(x)=\chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\cos kx,\qquad
\chi(u)=\frac{u\cosh u}{u^{2}+\cosh^{2}u}
       =\Big[\frac{u}{\cosh u}+\frac{\cosh u}{u}\Big]^{-1}>0,
\end{equation*}
$u=kh$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(u)=u+O(u^{3}),\;u\ll1,\qquad
\chi(u)\sim 2u\,e^{-u}\to0,\;u\gg1,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\chi_{\max}\simeq0.45\!-\!0.47\quad{\rm at}\quad kh\simeq1.0\!-\!1.2,
\end{equation*}
and for the geologically relevant $kh=O(1)$ mode

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\chi_{0}\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi_{0}\approx\frac12,
\end{equation*}
the familiar viscous–isostatic dynamic topography
$e_{s}\approx S_{0}/2\Delta\rho g$ with the usual $90^{\circ}$ phase
shift between the basal shear $\propto\sin kx$ and the surface
topography $\propto\cos kx$.  The long–wave lubrication limit is
$\chi\simeq kh$, i.e. $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, and the
$u/[\cosh u+u\sinh u]$ form is the same physics written with the
conventional impermeable–base normalisation $F(h)=0$; it coincides with
$\chi(u)$ to $O(u)$ for $u\ll1$.  The often quoted $O(1)$ estimate is
hence $\chi\approx1/2$ for $kh\sim1$, viz. $e_{s}\approx
S_{0}/2\Delta\rho g$ up to the conventional sign. ∎

